ed out of out of college you  didn't and then you had two kids by the  age of twenty and the people were  looking at you going I don't know about  Todd yeah no I was pretty lost at that  we relied on welfare to take care of my  two kids it was actually my dad who gave  me this piece of advice that changed  everything he said if I wanted something  better I had to figure out what really  motivated me and I had to stay close to  that and that advice changed my path  completely and that letting me get a GED  go to college and eventually end up at  Harvard where I get to study what makes  people take and you write about the game  of judgment that helps people find their  true motivations what is that you know  it's just a simple way to figure out you  know what motivates you and let me give  you the short version here you give out  the things that you enjoy doing and ask  yourself why right so if you enjoy  football is it the competition is it  being outdoors is it the camaraderie  that comes with team sports the more you  think about those things the more you  know what really moves you and if you  ask yourself that question off enough it  will reveal your broader motives and  that will put you on a path to  fulfillment and you see no such thing as  one size fits all fulfillment which I  thought was an interesting concept  ya know look we've tried that right  everybody's different and as soon as we  start appreciating that we can build  systems that allow everyone to be  successful and happy speaking of  building those systems how do you do  this you know scale way other than just  person by person you know what I think  twenty years ago it would have been  really hard to do now if you take  something like education we absolutely  can personalize it right we can take our  kids individuality seriously which I  find sort of odd is that we've figured  out how to personalize almost all other  aspects of our life but when it comes to  our kids education  we're shockingly sort of okay with the  status quo but we shouldn't be and so  how can parents encourage their kids and  let them live their full potential  without giving them what they need in  the life lessons that they need look I  think it comes down to one really  important thing especially when they're  young which is they need to figure out  what really matters to them and what  motivates them and we can help them by  asking but you think about as parents  we actually don't ask our kids that very  often we spend a lot of time telling  them what should matter yeah I'm very  little time helping them figure it out  for themselves yeah you said people  always think you have to choose the  behavior between what you like or what  you must do and you're saying you should  really focus on what you like yeah and  if you prioritize good wait but when  you're trying to support yourself and  make a living and survive sometimes you  can't do what you like to do you know  and I think that actually this darkhorse  mindset I believe matters even more for  people who don't have a safety net right  the people who have to say hit a home  run on every choice they make because  there's no room for error right  because I think their knowing who you  are knowing what really motivates you  and know how to make choices off of that  can make all the difference in the world  well it's not just a popular Katy Perry  song now it's a must-read book as well  dark force great to have you here when  that was your Katy Perry song idea now  you know Todd Todd Rose thank you so  much and Dark Horse is on sale now
 

